/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:=
700">Ostpolitik</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Until Russia=E2=80=99s full-scale invasion=
 of Ukraine, there was a shared belief in Germany =E2=80=94 embraced by a l=
ong succession of chancellors, industry leaders and the public =E2=80=94 th=
at a Russia bound by trade would have too much at risk to enter into a conf=
lict with Europe. Doing business with Russia, the thinking went, was a win-=
win: It would make Germany safer and richer.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That policy h=
ad its origins in the Cold War, when the West German government ushered in =
a new policy of engagement with the Soviet Union that became known as Ostpo=
litik. Ostpolitik was a broad philosophy that included other aspects of the=
 relationship, too =E2=80=94 diplomacy, cultural contact. But one of its de=
fining pillars, which only grew in importance over time, became known as =
=E2=80=9CWandel durch Handel,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cchange through trade.=E2=
=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333=
;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I spoke to Liana Fix, who wrote a book about German=
y=E2=80=99s evolving Russia policy. Ostpolitik helped build trust between t=
he two sides, particularly during the Cold War, she said. The gas pipelines=
 constructed from the 1970s onward might have made the end of the Cold War =
more peaceful.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;co=
lor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But at the time, gas deals were accompanied=
 by deterrence. =E2=80=9CThe other piece of it was missiles pointing at Mos=
cow,=E2=80=9D Fix said.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia=
,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That changed after the Cold War. A=
 reunified Germany cut military spending. At the same time, it intensified =
its business and energy ties with Moscow.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Initially, there=
 was a belief that these ties would help transform post-Soviet Russia into =
a liberal democracy, Fix told me. But even when Germany=E2=80=99s political=
 establishment became disillusioned with Putin, it still held on to the bel=
ief that mutual interdependence would make it too costly for him to step ou=
t of line.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:=
#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It kept believing this after the invasion of Cr=
imea in 2014: Angela Merkel persuaded Barack Obama not to ship arms to Ukra=
ine and instead approved Nord Stream 2. Germany had leverage over Russia, t=
he thinking went, precisely </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">becaus=
e</span><span> it was buying so much Russian gas.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The oppo=
site turned out to be true. It was Putin who restricted the flow of gas, in=
 2021. The next year, he invaded Ukraine.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cel=
lpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=
