></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span>But if Trump=E2=80=99s goal is to end the war, one thing the Ge=
rman experience shows is that Putin is motivated by things other than busin=
ess deals. This has been an incredibly costly war for Russia and Putin has =
shown that he=E2=80=99s prepared to pay the price.</span></p><p style=3D"fo=
nt:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Not eve=
ryone agrees about why Putin invaded Ukraine. Some, like Stephen Walt at Ha=
rvard, argue that what incited Putin was the offer of a path toward NATO me=
mbership extended to Ukraine in 2008. Walt pointed me to the memo the U.S. =
ambassador to Moscow at the time, William Burns, sent to Washington that ye=
ar, warning that =E2=80=9CUkrainian entry into NATO is the brightest of all=
 red lines for the Russian elite (not just Putin).=E2=80=9D</span></p><p st=
yle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><spa=
n>=E2=80=9CFrom knuckle-draggers in the dark recesses of the Kremlin to Put=
in=E2=80=99s sharpest liberal critics, I have yet to find anyone who views =
Ukraine in NATO as anything other than a direct challenge to Russian intere=
sts,=E2=80=9D Burns wrote.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Others, like Fix, say that even=
 in the absence of that offer, Putin=E2=80=99s longstanding imperial ambiti=
ons in Ukraine mean he most likely would have invaded anyway.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>But people with very different views of what is motivating Putin still =
agree on one thing: business deals won=E2=80=99t be enough to lead him to g=
ive up. And worse, they could enable and embolden him.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>T=
